period [2]. This causes circadian rhythm oscillations in the body. This rhythm information is gradually trans­ mitted to peripheral clock tissues and organs, regulat­ ing several important physiological processes, including hormone secretion, energy metabolism, inflammation, immune response, and aging [3-7]. Full list of author information is available at the end of the article As a peripheral rhythm-regulating organ, the cardio­ vascular system is influenced by neurohumoral factors, electrophysiological characteristics, and energy metab­ olism. These factors are subject to regulation by both rhythm control center, autonomous circadian rhythms and peripheral oscillation-affected factors, including environmental light, temperature, feeding, oxygen lev­ els, sensory stimulation, wakefulness/sleep (includ­ ing sleep deprivation, sleep restriction, and shift work), and exercise [8-12]. A substantial body of research has demonstrated the pivotal role of the biological clock in the pathogenesis of a wide range of diseases, including metabolic syndromes, neoplasms, neurological disor­ ders, respiratory illnesses, cardiovascular conditions, and immune-inflammatory responses [13-15]. Nevertheless, the precise means of attaining a 24-h circadian rhythm within an organism, optimizing the efficacy of treatment • The Author(s) 2026. Open Access This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives 4.0 International License, which permits any non-commercial use, sharing, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if you modified the licensed material.",
