        "document_name": "pubmed_42902164_PMC13685291_The CML_esRAGE ratio as a potential biomarker of early diast.pdf",
        "document_content": "P Disease duration P \u03b2 (95% CI) -CML/esRAGE 0.700(0.020,1.381) 0.044 - -0.004(-0.008,-0.001) 0.027 - (P < 0.05). AGEs are formed via non-enzymatic glyca­ tion (Maillard reaction) between reducing sugars and proteins, CML being the most abundant and well-char­ acterized species [22]. The observed higher serum CML in the T1D group is consistent with the effects of chronic hyperglycemia and the metabolic memory phenomenon, whereby AGEs may continue to accumulate even after glycemic control is achieved [23]. Methylglyoxal (MG) is a highly reactive dicarbonyl metabolite of glycolysis that reacts with protein arginine residues to form MG-H1 [24]. In our study, MG-H1 lev­ els did not differ significantly between groups, which is inconsistent with some previous reports. Kilhovd et al. reported increased serum MG-H1 in patients with type 2 diabetes [25], and Karachalias et al. demonstrated increased MG-H1 adducts in the retina of streptozoto­ cin-induced diabetic rats [24]. In the present study, the mean MG-H1 level in the diabetic group was numerically higher than that in the control group, though the differ­ ence was not statistically significant, which may be attrib­ uted to the limited sample size of this pilot study. conditions. Reduced e(lat) may suggest impaired relax­ ation in diabetic patients. No significant change was observed in e(sep), which may be explained by differ­ ences in myocardial fiber architecture: longitudinal and circular fibers abundant in the lateral wall are sparsely distributed in the interventricular septum [31].",
