      "document_content": "Variables with P < 0.1 in univariate analysis, together with pre-specified clinical covariates (age, sex, HbA1c, disease duration, heart rate), were entered into stepwise multivariable linear regression analysis to identify independent predictors. To mitigate the risk of overfitting, the number of covariates included in the final model was limited following the standard 10:1 events per variable rule, which is widely accepted for exploratory clinical studies. Of note, for multiple com­ parisons, Bonferroni correction was applied to adjust the significance threshold for all correlation and regres­ sion analyses; uncorrected P values were also reported for full transparency, given the exploratory nature of this study. All correlation and regression analyses focused on early myocardial damage were performed exclusively in the type 1 diabetic cohort. This study was designed as an exploratory pilot study on novel biomarkers for diabetic cardiomyopathy; therefore, no a priori power calculation was performed. The sample size was determined with reference to previously published exploratory studies in the same field. For this exploratory research, a two-tailed P value < 0.05 was considered statistically significant, and P values between 0.05 and 0.10 were defined as border­ line significance, which was interpreted as suggestive of potential biological relevance requiring validation in larger cohorts. Comparison of segmental longitudinal strain (LS) and global longitudinal strain (GLS) Seven segmental systolic longitudinal strain (LS) and GLS in the diabetic group were lower than those in the normal group, more details refer to Table 2.",
