ion of 0.5 was applied when a study contained one or more zero cells. Heterogene­ ity was quantified using I2, and results were presented in forest plots. All analyses were conducted in Stata/ MP 18.0. For overall FCI outcomes reported by at least 10 cohorts, small-study effects were assessed visually using funnel plots and statistically using Egger's regres­ sion test; p < 0.05 was considered evidence of funnel-plot asymmetry. Statistical analysis Demographic and baseline characteristics were summa­ rized descriptively. For postnatal-death analyses, event counts and denominators were extracted exclusively among live-born infants with known postnatal vital sta­ tus. For each outcome, event counts were extracted for the intervention and comparator groups, and ORs with 95% CIs were pooled using random-effects (REML) models. Live birth and postnatal death among live-born infants were analyzed for overall FCI and by procedure type (FAV, FPV, and FASI) versus expectant management. BVC was analyzed separately for FAV and FPV and was not pooled across procedure types because these pro­ cedures address distinct cardiac pathologies and patient populations. Additional pairwise comparisons were per­ formed among technically successful, technically failed, and expectant-management groups for FAV. Overall FCI analyses included all attempted interventions regardless Results Study selection After full-text assessment, 72 reports were excluded. Twelve reports were included in the review, representing 11 independent study populations. Twelve reports were included in the review, representing 11 independent study populations. Eleven comparative reports were included in the primary comparative quan­ titative synthesis, comprising 775 fetuses, including 418 interventions and 357 controls (Fig. 1) [4, 5, 7, 12, 16-22]. Friedman et al. [23], an overlapping report, contributed only to the secondary successful-versus-unsuccessful",
