        "document_content": "Open Access Information sources A comprehensive literature search was conducted in PubMed/MEDLINE, Embase, Web of Science, and the Cochrane Library from database inception to 31 Decem­ ber 2025. Two reviewers performed the search indepen­ dently. To ensure completeness, the reference lists of all included studies and of relevant systematic reviews and meta-analyses were also screened manually. Any disagreements were resolved through discussion and consensus. Search strategy The search strategy combined Medical Subject Head­ ings (MeSH), Emtree terms, and free-text keywords. No publication-date or language restrictions were applied. Reviews, editorials, comments, and letters were excluded. The following terms were used: Study selection and data extraction Study selection and data extraction were performed inde­ pendently by two authors using the Rayyan web appli­ cation. Titles and abstracts of all retrieved records were screened initially, and any study considered potentially relevant by either reviewer proceeded to full-text assess­ ment. Full texts were then reviewed to determine eligi­ bility according to the predefined inclusion and exclusion criteria. Any discrepancies between reviewers were resolved by consultation with a third author. (fetal OR fetus) AND (cardiac OR heart) AND (inter­ vention OR procedure OR surgery OR therapy OR \"intrauterine intervention\" OR \"in utero intervention\" OR \"fetal cardiac intervention\" OR FCI) AND (\"aortic valvuloplasty\" OR \"pulmonary valvuloplasty\" OR \"atrial septoplasty\" OR \"balloon valvuloplasty\" OR \"fetal aortic valvuloplasty\" OR \"fetal pulmonary valvuloplasty\" OR FAV OR FPV OR FASI) NOT (review OR editorial OR comment OR letter). For all studies meeting eligibility criteria, data were extracted independently and in duplicate, including study design, population characteristics, type of fetal cardiac intervention, comparator group, and reported clini­ cal outcomes. Extracted outcomes included established measures of gestational and postnatal prognosis relevant to fetal cardiac intervention. Consensus was reached through discussion when differences occurred, ensuring accuracy and completeness of the final dataset. When necessary, authors were contacted to clarify ambiguous reporting or obtain missing information. Cases that did not report outcome data (e.g., BVC, live birth, or post­ natal death) were excluded from analyses of the corre­ sponding endpoint. The detailed search strategies for each database, includ­ ing subject headings (e.g., MeSH and Emtree), are pro­ vided in Appendix S1. Definitions of clinical outcomes and procedural stratification Clinical outcomes were defined prior to analysis to ensure consistency across studies. Technical success was defined according to each original publication; however, in general, it reflected successful balloon inflation across the target valve with improvement in antegrade flow. For FAV, this typically corresponded to balloon inflation across the aortic valve with demonstrable enhancement of antegrade aortic flow. BVC was defined as a post­ natal circulation supported by separate functional left and right ventricles. When multiple time points were reported, outcomes at hospital discharge were priori­ tized. If discharge data were unavailable, the outcome as reported in the study was used. When the timing of BVC assessment was not specified, the reported value was accepted as final. Live birth was defined as an infant alive at the time of delivery. Postnatal death referred to mor­ tality occurring between birth and hospital discharge.",
