        "document_content": "Statistical analysis Given the presence of a competing event (transplantation), a competing risks analysis was performed, considering death as the event of interest and transplantation as the competing event. Associations between covariates and mortality were evaluated using the Fine-Gray subdistribution hazards model, which es­ timates the effect of covariates on the cumulative incidence of the event of interest in the presence of competing risks. Multivariable models were adjusted for age, sex, and IABP use, and were intentionally parsimonious given the limited number of events to avoid overfitting. Results are presented as sub­ distribution hazard ratios (SHR) with 95% confidence intervals. The proportional hazards assumption was assessed by testing interactions between covariates and time; when violations were identified, time-dependent effects were modeled using interac­ tion terms with follow-up time, and results are reported as SHR for the baseline effect and for the time-varying component (TVC SHR). Given the advanced clinical status of the study population, particular attention was paid to avoid overadjustment. Variables included in multivariable models were selected based on clin­ ical relevance and statistical criteria, acknowledging the po­ tential overlap between frailty measures and markers of disease severity, which may share common underlying pathways.",
