        "document_content": "Open Access Eluted peptides were analyzed on an Orbitrap Explo­ ris 480 mass spectrometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific) equipped with a nano-electrospray ionization source. The spray voltage was set to 2.3 kV, and high-field asymmetric waveform ion mobility spectrometry was applied using compensation voltages of - 70 V and - 45 V. Full MS scans were acquired in the Orbitrap analyzer at a resolu­ tion of 60,000 across an m/z range of 400-1,200. Tandem MS/MS spectra were acquired at a resolution of 30,000 with the first mass fixed at m/z 110. TurboTMT mode was disabled to maintain compatibility with label-free quantification. The top 15 most intense precursor ions were selected for higher-energy collisional dissociation with a normalized collision energy of 27%, and dynamic exclusion was set to 30 s. The automatic gain control tar­ get was set to 75%, with an intensity threshold of 10,000 ions/s and a maximum injection time of 100 ms. During hospitalization, all participants provided writ­ ten informed consent permitting the use of their blood samples for non-genetic experimental analyses. Compre­ hensive clinical data, including demographic character­ istics, comorbidities, vital signs, laboratory parameters, echocardiographic measurements, and medication use, were systematically collected from electronic medical records. Peripheral venous blood samples were obtained, and plasma was isolated by centrifugation and stored at -80 °C until analysis. Samples were transported on dry ice to Jingjie PTM BioLabs (Hangzhou, China) for label-free quantitative proteomic profiling. To reduce the interference of highly abundant plasma proteins, such as albumin and immunoglobulins, on downstream mass spectrometry-based analyses, plasma samples were depleted using PierceTM Top 14 Abundant Protein Depletion Spin Columns (Thermo Scientific) in strict accordance with the manufacturer's protocol. Briefly, frozen plasma aliquots were thawed on ice and centrifuged at 12,000 × g for 10 min at 4 °C to remove cellular debris. The clarified supernatant was transferred to fresh tubes and subjected to high-abundance protein depletion. Protein concentrations in the depleted frac­ tions were quantified using a bicinchoninic acid (BCA) assay, with all standards and samples measured in techni­ cal triplicates (Additional file 1: Table S1). Equal amounts of total protein were subsequently used for enzymatic digestion to ensure comparability across samples. Deple­ tion efficiency and protein integrity were evaluated by sodium dodecyl sulfate-polyacrylamide gel electropho­ resis (SDS-PAGE) before and after depletion (Additional file 1: Figure S2), and only samples demonstrating satis­ factory depletion efficiency and intact protein profiles were advanced for LC-MS/MS analysis. Samples with visible hemolysis or flagged as having a high risk of pre-analytical bias were excluded. Raw MS/MS data were processed using Proteome Dis­ coverer software (version 2.4.1.15). Spectra were searched against the Homo_sapiens_9606_PR_20201214.fasta database (75,777 entries), concatenated with a reverse decoy database to enable false discovery rate (FDR) esti­ mation. Trypsin was specified as the proteolytic enzyme, allowing up to two missed cleavages. The minimum pep­ tide length was set to six amino acids, and up to three variable modifications per peptide were permitted. Pre­ cursor mass tolerance was set at 10 ppm, and fragment ion tolerance at 0.02 Da.",
