      "document_id": "419e6d40-2bea-41d5-85f4-434a7e870cf1",
      "document_name": "pubmed_42902283_PMC13685146_Fetal cardiac interventions versus expectant management in c.pdf",
      "document_content": "Similar uncertainties exist for FASI and FPV in their respective indications [3, 4, 12]. Previous meta-analyses frequently relied on single-arm studies without an internal control group or on non- arm-based comparisons, in which outcomes for FCI and expectant management were extracted from different studies [3, 13-15]. Such approaches are inherently vul­ nerable to confounding and cannot adequately account for differences in baseline disease severity, case selection, and centre-specific practice patterns. To address these gaps, we conducted a primary meta-analysis restricted to cohorts in which FCI and expect­ ant management were compared within the same study population. A separate exploratory technical-success analysis additionally included FAV cohorts with extract­ able successful-versus-unsuccessful outcomes. Outcomes were pooled across independent cohorts to obtain more precise comparative estimates. To our knowledge, this is the first meta-analysis restricted to within-study com­ parisons of FCI and expectant management and the first quantitative synthesis of FAV outcomes according to We excluded case reports, conference abstracts, retracted publications, and studies in which outcome data for the relevant fetal cardiac condition could not be extracted separately. Overlapping reports that did not contribute unique analysis-specific data were excluded. When an overlapping report provided eligible data for a distinct secondary analysis, it was retained as an included report only for that analysis, did not add an independent study population, and was not pooled simultaneously with the overlapping report. stratified by procedure type (FAV, FPV, or FASI). For FAV, pairwise subgroup comparisons were conducted between (i) technical success vs. expectant management, (ii) tech­ nical failure vs. expectant management, and (iii) technical success vs. technical failure, where data were available. For comparative meta-analyses, postnatal death was ana­ lyzed using the number of live-born infants with known postnatal vital status in each study arm as the denomina­ tor. Fetuses that did not result in live birth were excluded from the postnatal-death risk set. Outcome availability was assessed separately for each study arm and endpoint. Studies or study arms lacking extractable comparative data for a given outcome were excluded only from that endpoint-specific meta-analysis. Cohort-level numera­ tors, denominators, and reasons for non-inclusion in individual outcome analyses are reported in Table 1.",
